<template>
  <main class="case-study">
    <header class="topbar">
      <a class="brand" :href="homeUrl">Bihan Zhang</a>
      <a class="back-link" :href="`${homeUrl}#research-projects`"><span aria-hidden="true">&larr;</span> Back to research</a>
    </header>

    <article>
      <section class="hero shell">
        <div class="eyebrow">Embodied HRI &amp; Intelligent-Cockpit Interaction · Independent Design Research · 2022</div>
        <h1>Proactive Embodied Voice Assistant for Safety-Critical Driving</h1>
        <p class="lede">Exploring how speech, text, affective expression, and escalating alerts can work as one coherent social signal when an in-vehicle assistant needs to interrupt the driver.</p>
        <div class="chips"><span>Independent project</span><span>6 exploratory interviews</span><span>Proactive HRI</span><span>Multimodal feedback</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="shell two-column">
        <div><p class="kicker">Design question</p><h2>How should an embodied assistant communicate urgency without becoming distracting or socially abrasive?</h2></div>
        <div class="prose"><p>Most in-vehicle assistants wait for a command. Safety-critical driving creates a different interaction problem: the system may need to initiate contact, escalate a warning, and make the required action legible within seconds.</p><p>I explored whether a consistent character, progressive affect, and coordinated verbal and visual cues could make these interventions easier to interpret while preserving a sense of companionship.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="metrics-band"><div class="shell metric-grid"><div><strong>6</strong><span>exploratory semi-structured interviews</span></div><div><strong>4</strong><span>automotive assistant references compared</span></div><div><strong>28</strong><span>scenario mappings reviewed from earlier HMI work</span></div><div><strong>3 levels</strong><span>progressive alert escalation</span></div></div></section>

      <section class="lineage"><div class="shell lineage__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Project boundary</p><h2>Related experience, two distinct projects</h2><p class="prose">An earlier in-vehicle HMI project produced a detailed scenario specification and DMS interface concepts. The later 2022 portfolio was a separate independent project: it reframed selected driving situations around character, social signaling, and embodied feedback.</p></div><div class="lineage-flow"><article><span>Earlier project</span><strong>In-vehicle HMI specification</strong><p>Driver monitoring, automation states, speech, text, and interface responses</p></article><i aria-hidden="true">informed</i><article class="active"><span>2022 project</span><strong>Embodied assistant concept</strong><p>Persona, affect, escalation, and coherent multimodal behavior</p></article></div></div></section>

      <section class="shell persona">
        <div class="section-intro"><p class="kicker">Persona construction</p><h2>Build the assistant from interaction responsibilities—not decoration</h2><p class="prose">Competitive review and exploratory interviews were translated into three connected design layers. Together they define who the assistant is, how it behaves, and how that behavior remains recognizable across changing levels of urgency.</p></div>
        <div class="persona-map" role="img" aria-label="Persona construction framework flowing from research inputs through identity, temperament, and expression strategy to an embodied assistant persona">
          <div class="input-node"><small>Research inputs</small><strong>Competitive review + exploratory interviews</strong></div>
          <div class="connector"><span>translate into</span></div>
          <div class="pillar-grid">
            <article><span>01 · Identity</span><h3>What is it?</h3><ul><li>Name and apparent age</li><li>Gender expression</li><li>2D, 3D, or physical embodiment</li><li>Abstract, animal-like, or human-like form</li></ul></article>
            <article><span>02 · Temperament</span><h3>How does it feel?</h3><ul><li>Warm rather than authoritative</li><li>Active but not intrusive</li><li>Calm in routine moments</li><li>Firm when risk escalates</li></ul></article>
            <article><span>03 · Expression</span><h3>How does it act?</h3><ul><li>Voice and tone</li><li>Language style</li><li>Affective expression</li><li>Interaction and escalation strategy</li></ul></article>
          </div>
          <div class="connector"><span>converge as</span></div>
          <div class="output-node"><small>Resulting persona</small><strong>A warm, expressive, non-humanoid companion that becomes progressively more direct as risk increases</strong></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="use-cases"><div class="shell"><div class="use-cases__header"><div><p class="kicker">Scenario vocabulary</p><h2>Selected use cases from the earlier HMI specification</h2></div><p class="prose">The spreadsheet contained 28 mappings. Rather than reproduce it as a screenshot, the representative scenarios below show the design logic that was later reconsidered through an embodied-agent lens.</p></div>
        <div class="scenario-grid">
          <article v-for="scenario in scenarios" :key="scenario.code"><div class="scenario-card__head"><span>{{ scenario.code }}</span><strong>{{ scenario.title }}</strong></div><p>{{ scenario.trigger }}</p><div class="state-row"><span v-for="state in scenario.states" :key="state">{{ state }}</span></div><small>{{ scenario.response }}</small></article>
        </div>
      </div></section>

      <section class="shell grammar"><div><p class="kicker">Interaction grammar</p><h2>Separate what happened from how the assistant should respond</h2></div><div class="grammar-flow"><article><span>01</span><strong>Context trigger</strong><p>DMS, automation, environment, or cabin state</p></article><i>→</i><article><span>02</span><strong>Severity</strong><p>Advisory, warning, or action-critical</p></article><i>→</i><article><span>03</span><strong>Social behavior</strong><p>Temperament, directness, and affect</p></article><i>→</i><article><span>04</span><strong>Output channels</strong><p>Speech, text, expression, and placement</p></article></div></section>

      <section class="escalation"><div class="shell escalation__inner"><div><p class="kicker">Progressive embodiment</p><h2>One character, three levels of interruption</h2><p class="prose">Repeated alerts should not simply repeat. The assistant changes its wording, affect, and visual salience as the situation becomes more urgent.</p></div><div class="level-grid"><article><span>Level 1</span><h3>Notice</h3><p>Gentle tone, compact expression, and a concise advisory.</p><small>“You look tired. Please stay refreshed.”</small></article><article><span>Level 2</span><h3>Warn</h3><p>Firmer tone, concerned affect, and an explicit behavioral request.</p><small>“Your drowsiness is getting worse. Stay awake.”</small></article><article class="critical"><span>Level 3</span><h3>Act</h3><p>High-salience expression and a direct safety-critical instruction.</p><small>“Do not fall asleep. Take over immediately.”</small></article></div></div></section>

      <section class="shell opportunity"><div><p class="kicker">Research opportunity</p><h2>A foundation for a focused HRI study</h2></div><div class="opportunity-list"><p><span>Embodiment</span>Compare voice-only feedback with voice plus an expressive visual agent.</p><p><span>Escalation</span>Test whether adaptive expressions improve urgency comprehension without increasing annoyance.</p><p><span>Calibration</span>Measure whether the assistant helps drivers distinguish advisory, warning, and takeover-critical states.</p><p><span>Behavior</span>Evaluate response accuracy, response time, perceived urgency, trust calibration, and interruption cost.</p></div></section>

      <section class="disclosure"><div class="shell disclosure__inner"><strong>Evidence boundary</strong><p>This page documents an independent exploratory design project completed in 2022 and clearly separates it from the earlier HMI specification. It does not claim implementation of a DMS algorithm, a controlled trust or workload experiment, or statistically generalizable findings.</p></div></section>
    </article>

    <footer class="footer shell"><p>Interested in this research?</p><a href="mailto:Zhangbihan621@outlook.com">Zhangbihan621@outlook.com</a><a class="back-link" :href="homeUrl">Return to Bihan Zhang’s academic website</a></footer>
  </main>
